    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, avoiding \"Unknown Person\" to prevent factual errors. But what about cases where the map is clearly the work of an intentional \"anonymous\" entity, or where the style is so distinct and mysterious that it feels like it belongs to a certain group, but we just don't have names? In those instances, is there any way to use controlled vocabularies or specific notation to capture that sense of a mysterious or \"hidden\" origin without just leaving it blank? I want to capture the character of the collection.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can use descriptor lists or scope/notes to describe the \"anonymous\" nature rather than using a formal DC element for the creator itself, as controlled vocabularies generally don't have a standard term for \"mysterious\" origins. Instead, consider adding descriptive notes or utilizing the 'hasPart' or related element to link blank maps to a specific style or group defined in your own authority file. This approach preserves the integrity of the metadata schema while still communicating the distinct character of your collection to researchers.\n\nCombining this with fields like 'description' or 'adkey' allows you to hint at potential authors or stylistic groups without making false attribution claims. Ultimately, this metadata strategy prioritizes transparency about the uncertainty of authorship while giving your collection context that is lost if fields remain completely empty.",
